investigations/prompt-token-attribution/REPORT.html:143:<h3>6. count_tokens: independent counts; the log is enough</h3>
investigations/prompt-token-attribution/REPORT.html:144:<p>The request rebuilt from one session file reproduces the real prompt size of the first call. System blocks + inline tools + first user message (all counted with <code>count_tokens</code>) + the <code>system</code>-role blob that holds the environment text give <strong>24700 against the real 24702</strong> (<code>pre-default</code>). The same −2 residual holds for <code>base</code>, <code>user-w200</code>, <code>user-w800</code>, <code>claudemd-w800</code>, <code>skills-listing-3</code>, <code>pre-tools-none</code> (4019 vs 4021), <code>pre-tools-bash</code> and <code>pre-floor</code> (657 vs 659). Second calls of the tool scenarios match within 3 tokens.</p>
investigations/prompt-token-attribution/REPORT.html:172:  <li><strong>Per-call input from the stream alone is not possible</strong>: usage gives one growth for all three results. Splitting needs the text of each result from the session file and <code>count_tokens</code> (or the tokens-per-word slope, 2.14, for a rough share: 14, 229, 1725 here, which scale with the words).</li>
investigations/prompt-token-attribution/REPORT.html:202:  <li>The preamble can be itemized in tokens from one finished conversation: the session file holds the text of every piece, and counting it with <code>count_tokens</code> reproduces the first call's prompt size to within 2 tokens of 24702. Without <code>count_tokens</code>, leave-one-out runs with flags give the same split within 0.2%; a chars-to-tokens ratio does not, because it varies by content (0.31 to 0.50).</li>
